<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sabium</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. c. 1844–1831 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century-bce">19th century BCE</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-babylonian-empire">Old Babylonian Empire</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sabium was an early king of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-babylonian-empire">First Dynasty of Babylon</a> in the nineteenth century BCE. He was one of the five rulers, from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumuabum">Sumuabum</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sin-muballit">Sin-muballit</a>, who preceded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hammurabi">Hammurabi</a>. Accounts of the Babylonian Empire describe these kings as having slowly built <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a> from a minor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/city-state">city-state</a> into a recognised regional power by completing its walls and developing its infrastructure.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Sabium, also written Sabum or Zabium, was an Amorite king of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-babylonian-empire">First Dynasty of Babylon</a>, the Amorite Dynasty. He ruled a small, recently created Amorite kingdom that included the town of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a>. He made no claim to kingship of Babylon itself, which suggests that it had not yet grown into the major metropolis it would become under his descendant <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hammurabi">Hammurabi</a>. He kept control of the city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sippar">Sippar</a>, conquered by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumu-la-el">Sumu-la-El</a>. He was roughly contemporary with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/enlil-bani">Enlil-bani</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isin">Isin</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sin-iddinam">Sin-Iddinam</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/larsa">Larsa</a>, Ibal-pi-El I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eshnunna">Eshnunna</a> and the obscure Aminum of Ekallatum.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Because the Old Babylonian levels at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a> lie below the water table and were not excavated, what is known of Sabium comes from other sites, such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sippar">Sippar</a>, which Babylon controlled. About 14 of his year names are known. They record the destruction of the city wall of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kazallu">Kazallu</a>, the smiting of the army of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/larsa">Larsa</a>, the building of the city wall of Kar-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/utu">Shamash</a>, the building of the temple <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/e-babbar">E-babbar</a> of Shamash, the restoration of the temple of Ibbi-Anum and the building of E-sagil.</p><p>Ibal-pi-El I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eshnunna">Eshnunna</a> concluded a treaty with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sin-iddinam">Sin-Iddinam</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/larsa">Larsa</a> and Sîn-kāšid of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uruk">Uruk</a> against Sabium and his ally Ikun-pi-Sin of Nerebtum. In his accession year, 1844 BCE, Sabium decreed a misharum, a cancellation of debt, a fairly common practice for rulers of the period. The god Nabu is first attested in the pantheon of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a> in his reign.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>In a letter to the later Babylonian ruler Sin-Muballit, An-am of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uruk">Uruk</a>, who ruled around 1824 to 1816 BCE, reminded him that Sabium had sent a thousand soldiers to support Uruk in his time. A Neo-Babylonian text contains a literary chronicle of Sabium and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apil-sin">Apil-Sin</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sabium” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>For a century, five successive rulers, Sumu-abum, Sumu-la-El, <b>Sabium</b>, Apil-Sin and Sin-muballit, slowly turned this minor city into a recognised regional power.</blockquote><a href="/empire/babylonian-empire">Babylonian Empire · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
